1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,359
Every masterclass ends the same way. With proof.

2
00:00:03,909 --> 00:00:08,026
Not a quiz. A broken network, and four checks that say pass or fail.

3
00:00:08,576 --> 00:00:13,077
When the checks pass, anyone can see that you fixed it. That is mastery evidence.

4
00:00:13,627 --> 00:00:15,252
Today you build the scenario,

5
00:00:15,252 --> 00:00:17,101
fix the first fault live with me,

6
00:00:17,101 --> 00:00:18,726
and learn how to document it.

7
00:00:20,776 --> 00:00:25,384
Here is the scenario. A small shop service, called cart, runs on a server.

8
00:00:25,934 --> 00:00:28,430
A client has to reach it, through one router.

9
00:00:28,980 --> 00:00:30,747
Two addresses must work.

10
00:00:30,747 --> 00:00:33,398
Plain H T T P on port eighty eighty,

11
00:00:33,398 --> 00:00:37,300
and secure H T T P S on port eighty four forty three.

12
00:00:37,850 --> 00:00:41,370
Four things are broken. I will not say which. The checker will.

13
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:43,392
You need a Linux machine,

14
00:00:43,392 --> 00:00:45,924
the lab scripts from the course repository,

15
00:00:45,924 --> 00:00:47,573
and an hour without rushing.

16
00:00:49,623 --> 00:00:51,049
I already built the lab.

17
00:00:51,049 --> 00:00:52,119
It makes a client,

18
00:00:52,119 --> 00:00:53,426
a router and a server,

19
00:00:53,426 --> 00:00:54,792
and breaks four things.

20
00:00:54,792 --> 00:00:56,040
It takes ten seconds,

21
00:00:56,040 --> 00:00:58,476
and you will run it yourself in a moment.

22
00:00:59,026 --> 00:01:04,232
Now ask the checker. Type: lab check. Lab is my short name for the lab script.

23
00:01:09,782 --> 00:01:10,535
Four checks,

24
00:01:10,535 --> 00:01:11,226
four fails.

25
00:01:11,226 --> 00:01:12,985
That is your starting point.

26
00:01:12,985 --> 00:01:15,435
Every fix must turn a fail into a pass.

27
00:01:17,485 --> 00:01:21,453
Before you touch anything, a rule. For every fault, you keep four notes.

28
00:01:22,003 --> 00:01:24,350
One: the exact message, before the fix.

29
00:01:24,900 --> 00:01:27,396
Two: the cause, and the command that proves it.

30
00:01:27,946 --> 00:01:29,631
Three: the one change you made.

31
00:01:30,181 --> 00:01:32,037
Four: the proof, after the fix.

32
00:01:32,587 --> 00:01:34,394
Those four notes are your report.

33
00:01:34,394 --> 00:01:35,708
They take a minute each,

34
00:01:35,708 --> 00:01:37,515
and they turn luck into evidence.

35
00:01:39,565 --> 00:01:40,663
Fault one,

36
00:01:40,663 --> 00:01:41,212
live.

37
00:01:41,212 --> 00:01:43,739
Check two failed: ping.

38
00:01:43,739 --> 00:01:45,935
Start with evidence.

39
00:01:45,935 --> 00:01:49,560
Type: cl ping -c 1 -W 1 10.0.2.2.

40
00:01:49,560 --> 00:01:53,624
C L runs a command inside the client.

41
00:01:54,174 --> 00:01:54,764
No reply,

42
00:01:54,764 --> 00:01:56,140
and no error message.

43
00:01:56,140 --> 00:01:56,664
Silence.

44
00:01:56,664 --> 00:01:58,171
The client has a route,

45
00:01:58,171 --> 00:02:01,448
so something on the way is not passing packets on.

46
00:02:01,998 --> 00:02:11,364
Where does the path stop? Type: cl traceroute -n -w 1 -q 1 -m 3 10.0.2.2

47
00:02:11,494 --> 00:02:15,632
Stars from the very first hop. The gateway is not forwarding traffic.

48
00:02:16,182 --> 00:02:22,988
Ask the router. Type: rt sysctl net.ipv4.ip_forward

49
00:02:23,118 --> 00:02:26,147
Zero. Forwarding is switched off. That is the cause.

50
00:02:26,697 --> 00:02:35,636
One change only. Type: rt sysctl -w net.ipv4.ip_forward=1

51
00:02:35,766 --> 00:02:42,998
Now the proof. Type: cl ping -c 1 -W 1 10.0.2.2

52
00:02:43,128 --> 00:02:47,202
A reply. The server is reachable. Save this output in your report.

53
00:02:48,252 --> 00:02:50,834
Run the checker again. Type: lab check

54
00:02:53,464 --> 00:02:54,691
Check two is a pass.

55
00:02:54,691 --> 00:02:55,734
Three still fail.

56
00:02:55,734 --> 00:02:58,435
Notice that the name check is still failing,

57
00:02:58,435 --> 00:03:00,337
and it needed the router first.

58
00:03:00,337 --> 00:03:01,933
Fix the lower layer first.

59
00:03:03,983 --> 00:03:09,167
Now the report. There is a template in the repository, with one section per fault.

60
00:03:09,717 --> 00:03:11,893
Symptom: the exact message you saw.

61
00:03:12,443 --> 00:03:14,640
Cause: what was wrong, in one sentence.

62
00:03:15,190 --> 00:03:17,622
Fix: the single command or edit you made.

63
00:03:18,172 --> 00:03:20,050
Proof: the output after the fix.

64
00:03:20,600 --> 00:03:24,589
At the end, paste the full checker output, with all four lines passing.

65
00:03:25,139 --> 00:03:27,998
Fill it in as you go, not at the end from memory.

66
00:03:30,048 --> 00:03:33,269
Your turn begins where I stopped. Three checks still fail.

67
00:03:33,819 --> 00:03:36,955
The name. The plain web page. The secure web page.

68
00:03:37,505 --> 00:03:38,900
Each has its own clue,

69
00:03:38,900 --> 00:03:40,295
just like last lesson.

70
00:03:40,295 --> 00:03:41,563
Open the clue table,

71
00:03:41,563 --> 00:03:44,225
and read the exact words of every message.

72
00:03:44,775 --> 00:03:47,975
Fix one at a time, and run the checker after each fix.

73
00:03:48,525 --> 00:03:51,158
The lab script has three helpers: client,

74
00:03:51,158 --> 00:03:52,314
router and server.

75
00:03:52,314 --> 00:03:55,139
Each one runs a command inside that machine.

76
00:03:55,689 --> 00:03:56,639
If you are stuck,

77
00:03:56,639 --> 00:03:58,764
ask which layer fails first: the name,

78
00:03:58,764 --> 00:03:59,323
the route,

79
00:03:59,323 --> 00:03:59,826
the port,

80
00:03:59,826 --> 00:04:00,553
or the trust.

81
00:04:02,603 --> 00:04:07,680
When you are done, clean up. A good engineer leaves nothing behind. Type: lab down

82
00:04:07,810 --> 00:04:13,293
Lab removed. Now prove it. Type: ip netns list | wc -l

83
00:04:13,423 --> 00:04:15,450
Zero network namespaces left.

84
00:04:15,999 --> 00:04:21,525
And no servers running. Type: pgrep -fc web25.py

85
00:04:21,655 --> 00:04:24,258
Zero. Put that proof at the end of your report.

86
00:04:26,308 --> 00:04:30,489
Here is how your scenario is judged. Pass or fail, no opinions.

87
00:04:31,039 --> 00:04:33,087
One: the checker shows four passes.

88
00:04:33,637 --> 00:04:37,755
Two: your report has symptom, cause, fix and proof for every fault.

89
00:04:38,305 --> 00:04:42,251
Three: you changed one thing at a time, and the report shows the order.

90
00:04:42,801 --> 00:04:46,961
Four: the cleanup proof shows no namespaces and no servers left.

91
00:04:47,511 --> 00:04:50,583
Miss one, and you are not done yet. Go back and fix it.

92
00:04:51,133 --> 00:04:53,421
Also write down where you got stuck.

93
00:04:53,421 --> 00:04:54,693
That is not failure.

94
00:04:54,693 --> 00:04:57,299
It is the most useful part of the report.

95
00:04:59,349 --> 00:05:01,952
Your turn. Do this in your own virtual machine.

96
00:05:02,502 --> 00:05:07,460
One: copy the three lab files from the course repository into user local bin.

97
00:05:07,460 --> 00:05:09,456
The link is in the description.

98
00:05:10,006 --> 00:05:14,102
Two: run the lab script with sudo, and the word up. Wait ten seconds.

99
00:05:14,652 --> 00:05:17,468
Three: run check, and save the four fail lines.

100
00:05:18,018 --> 00:05:21,304
Four: fix all four faults, one at a time, with your notes.

101
00:05:21,854 --> 00:05:24,862
Five: run check again, and save the four passes.

102
00:05:25,412 --> 00:05:28,526
Six: clean up with lab down, and save that proof too.

103
00:05:29,076 --> 00:05:30,783
Pause the video, and start now.

104
00:05:46,333 --> 00:05:47,208
Quick check.

105
00:05:47,758 --> 00:05:49,251
How many checks must pass?

106
00:05:49,801 --> 00:05:51,870
Which four notes do you keep for each fault?

107
00:05:52,420 --> 00:05:53,807
What does the cleanup prove?

108
00:05:54,357 --> 00:05:56,362
Why did we fix the router before the name?

109
00:05:56,912 --> 00:05:59,920
Mastery evidence for masterclass three is complete.

110
00:06:00,470 --> 00:06:02,540
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

111
00:06:13,090 --> 00:06:17,207
Next masterclass: M C zero four, Linux administration.

112
00:06:17,757 --> 00:06:21,085
If this helped, like, share and subscribe, so you do not miss it.
